    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop a portable single-threaded baseline that combines sparse row-wise blockers with an in-place DP scan; investigate fast input and ARM64-friendly prefix-scan/vectorization while preserving duplicate/start/destination blocker correctness.",
        "B": "Investigate a correctness-safe hybrid algorithm: dense in-place row DP for small/moderate n and combinatorial obstacle DP for sparse, very large grids. Define an input-dependent crossover without specializing to benchmark values.",
        "C": "Refine the measured best OpenMP segmented prefix-scan approach for 4 ARM64 cores: remove avoidable synchronization/work, verify carry propagation at segment blockers and n=1, and compare compact blocker layouts against the dense bitmap.",
        "G": "Integrate only measured improvements, starting from C's 0.02288s candidate. Test combinations of OpenMP segmented DP and sparse/row-oriented blocking; retain the fastest correct whole program rather than assuming component gains add.",
        "analysis": "The baseline performs quadratic dense-grid work plus a row copy. Measured wins show in-place DP is essential; C's 4-core segmented scan is currently best (0.02288s), narrowly ahead of B's sparse NEON implementation (0.02524s). Dense and sparse formulations have different asymptotic/memory tradeoffs under the stated broad constraints, so research should preserve correctness across both rather than tune to a single observed input. The quadratic obstacle predecessor proposal is slower than baseline and should not be integrated unchanged."
